---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import FeatureHero from '../../components/FeatureHero.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/Prose.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CtaSection from '../../components/CtaSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../site.config';

const title = 'Proxy & RBAC';
const description = 'Built-in Kroxylicious proxy enforces OIDC / JWT-claim-based RBAC at the wire. Topic, consumer-group, and schema-registry ACLs in one CR. mTLS internal; per-user quotas.';

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What client auth methods are supported?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'SASL/OAUTHBEARER (OIDC + JWT) and mTLS. The proxy also supports SASL/PLAIN handshake synthesis so legacy clients work with a JWT.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How do groups map to roles?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'A custom Kroxylicious filter extracts the JWT, reads realm_access.roles by default (configurable), and intersects against the topic / group / schema rules in the KafkaRbac CR.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Can I still use plain Kafka ACLs?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes. The proxy is opt-in. Without it, clients hit brokers directly and standard ACLs apply.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Where does the JWT come from?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Any OIDC provider. The reference setup uses Keycloak with realm-based roles; production deployments routinely use Auth0, Azure AD, Okta.' } },
  ],
};

const rbacYaml = `apiVersion: kafka.yavari.afshin.se/v1alpha1
kind: KafkaRbac
metadata:
  name: prod-rbac
spec:
  groups:
    - name: orders-team
      kafka:
        topics: [orders, orders-dlq]
        consumerGroups: [orders-svc-*]
        operations: [PRODUCE, FETCH]
      schemaRegistry:
        artifacts: [orders, orders-dlq]
        actions: [READ, WRITE]
    - name: schema-admin
      schemaRegistry:
        artifacts: ["*"]
        actions: [READ, WRITE, DELETE]
  users:
    - name: app1                # mTLS cert CN
      kafka:
        topics: [orders]
        operations: [PRODUCE]
      quotas:
        producerByteRate: 1048576    # 1 MiB/s
        requestPercentage: 25
`;

const proxyYaml = `apiVersion: kafka.yavari.afshin.se/v1alpha1
kind: KafkaCluster
metadata:
  name: prod
spec:
  proxy:
    enabled: true
    replicas: 2
    clientPort: 9094
    rbacRef: prod-rbac
    oidc:
      jwksEndpointUrl: https://sso.example.com/realms/prod/protocol/openid-connect/certs
      groupsClaim: realm_access.roles
    externalAccess:
      mode: LOADBALANCER          # also: NODEPORT / GATEWAY / INGRESS
      tlsSecretRef: kafka-public-tls
`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={faqSchema}>
  <FeatureHero
    eyebrow="Proxy & RBAC"
    title="Kafka RBAC that thinks in JWTs, not ACLs."
    lede="A built-in Kroxylicious proxy intercepts every client request, verifies the JWT, and authorizes against topic, consumer-group, and schema-registry rules declared in a single CR. Same JWT — same identity — across Kafka and the registry."
    meta={['Kroxylicious 0.21', 'SASL/OAUTHBEARER', 'mTLS internal', 'per-user quotas']}
  />

  <section class="container-site py-16">
    <Prose>
      <h2 id="problem">Native Kafka ACLs are not enough.</h2>
      <p>
        Kafka ACLs identify clients by SASL principal or mTLS CN. They don't
        understand groups, organizational units, or OIDC claims. To run Kafka
        for more than a handful of teams, you end up with hundreds of ACL
        entries you maintain by hand and a stack of glue scripts to keep them
        in sync with your IdP.
      </p>
      <p>
        {site.brand} replaces that with a thin Kroxylicious proxy in front of
        the brokers. Every client connection presents a JWT (or an mTLS cert).
        The proxy maps claims to roles, matches roles against a declarative
        <code>KafkaRbac</code> CR, and forwards the request to the broker — or
        rejects it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works.</h2>
      <p>
        The proxy is two custom Kroxylicious filters layered over the stock
        request pipeline:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>JwtGroupFilter</code> extracts the JWT, validates it against the JWKS endpoint, and pulls <code>realm_access.roles</code> into a per-connection group set.</li>
        <li><code>GroupAwareAuthorizerService</code> checks every produce / fetch / topic / consumer-group operation against the rules compiled from <code>KafkaRbac</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The same JWT also authorizes schema-registry artifact access via the
        Apicurio RBAC proxy described in
        <a href="/features/schema-governance/">Schema governance</a>. One token,
        two enforcement points, one policy CR.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-10 max-w-prose">
      <CodeBlock code={rbacYaml} lang="yaml" filename="kafka-rbac.yaml" />
    </div>

    <Prose>
      <h3>Enable the proxy on the cluster.</h3>
      <p>
        The proxy is a sub-spec of <code>KafkaCluster</code>. Reference the
        <code>KafkaRbac</code> CR, point it at an OIDC issuer, pick an
        external-access mode.
      </p>
    </Prose>

    <div class="my-10 max-w-prose">
      <CodeBlock code={proxyYaml} lang="yaml" filename="kafka-cluster.yaml" />
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-prose">
      <Callout title="vs. Strimzi + Keycloak authorizer">
        <p>
          Strimzi can plug a Keycloak authorizer into brokers via JAAS — but
          you write and maintain the policy mapping yourself, you don't get a
          proxy layer, and you have no path to apply the same identity to the
          schema registry.
        </p>
        <p>
          {site.brand} ships the proxy, the filter stack, the policy compiler,
          and the registry integration as one operator. One CR.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>

    <Prose>
      <h3>Per-user quotas.</h3>
      <p>
        Rate-limiting lives in the same CR. Producer / consumer byte rates,
        request-handler percentage, and controller mutation rate are applied
        via <code>AdminClient.alterClientQuotas</code> on the primary cluster
        when the CR reconciles.
      </p>

      <h3>mTLS by default for everything internal.</h3>
      <p>
        Set <code>spec.proxyMtls: true</code> and broker INTERNAL listeners
        switch to SSL with a shared CA. The operator provisions PEM material
        and projects it via PKCS12 init containers — no manual cert work.
      </p>

      <h3 id="audit">Every decision is audited.</h3>
      <p>
        The same filter chain that authorises a request also emits a JSON
        audit line on the SLF4J <code>kafka-audit</code> channel — principal,
        groups, operation, resource, decision, latency, correlation id. The
        Apicurio RBAC proxy uses the same record format for schema-registry
        access, and kafka-editor closes the loop with per-write events on
        <code>kafka-editor.audit</code>. See <a href="/features/audit-logging/">audit logging</a> for the unified
        feed and sink options.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>

  <CtaSection />
</Base>
